Bean spend went up 123 percent, pero el precio only up 40 percent. Entonces volume: 2.23 divided by 1.40 = 1.59. So pounds of beans consumed are up about 59 percent, while revenue is only up 15 percent. Pounds per dollar of revenue up roughly 38 percent — 1.59 over 1.15.

So there are two distinct problems, and I'd been conflating them. One is external: a 40 percent commodity spike, with no pricing action taken. Two is internal: they're using dramatically more beans per unit sold. That's over-portioning, waste, spoilage, calibration on the grinders, or a mix shift toward higher-dose drinks.

The second one is the more interesting finding because it's controllable without touching price.

