[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 8:05] I don't have drink mix or waste and shrinkage data.

You've isolated two drivers. Beyond what we've discussed, what could the client do to reverse the decline? Give me structure.

[candidate · 9:35] I'll bucket it three ways: price, input cost, and consumption efficiency — plus overhead as a smaller fourth.

On price: prices haven't moved in two years while everything inflated. Even a modest increase is high leverage. A 5 percent price increase on revenue of 115 is about 5.75 dropping almost straight to profit, entonces from 6.9 to roughly 12.6 — that nearly doubles profit on its own, assuming volume holds. Specialty coffee is fairly inelastic, but I'd test in a subset of the 200 stores.

On input cost: hedge or forward-contract beans, renegotiate with suppliers at 200-store scale, consider a second origin to reduce exposure to the 40 percent spike.

On consumption: this is the 38 percent efficiency gap. Standardize dosing, scale calibration, portion audits, track waste by store and benchmark — the spread across stores will show how much is recoverable.

On overhead: two points of growth, worth a look but not the priority.

I'd want the waste data to size bucket three.
